{
  "image_name": "0e7cc5cfe0ea8a16463dee6ec3a7665d.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how teplizumab is proposed to protect pancreatic β-cells by altering T-cell behavior over time. In both panels, a T cell engages an antigen-presenting cell through the TCR/CD3 complex, and teplizumab binds CD3, ultimately reducing β-cell killing, shown by the blocked arrow toward the β-cell.\n\nThe left side highlights early effects. The T cell is marked with molecules such as TIGIT and KLRG1 and the transcription factor EOMES, suggesting a shift toward an altered or partially exhausted state. The accompanying notes indicate an initial drop in lymphocytes, followed by an increase in CD8+ T cells and a change in the CD4+:CD8+ ratio. Overall, the early response seems to dampen destructive immune activity while reshaping the T-cell compartment.\n\nThe right side shows longer-term effects. Here the key visible change is increased PD1 expression on the T cell, with text indicating this occurs on central memory and anergic CD8+ T cells. This implies a more sustained inhibitory or less aggressive T-cell phenotype. Taken together, the figure conveys that teplizumab does not simply block T cells acutely, but reprograms them into less harmful states that help preserve β-cells.",
